14.29.m:
Then that king immediately became one with a terrifying face—
a tiger, ten cubits long, with sharp teeth and claws;
and fainting with rage, he devoured his own army.

Kapilā said:
"I do not weep in any way out of fear for my own life, O tiger;
my young son, who drinks milk, is waiting in the cowpen.

14.30.m:
Pulastya said:
Then one should go to Agnitīrtha, which brings supreme purification to men;
there Fire was lost in ancient times, and recovered even by the gods.

Pulastya said:
In ancient times there was a cessation of rain for twelve years;
all the world was afflicted with hunger and faced extreme peril.

The gods said:
O Agni, you move within all beings, O purifier;
deprived of you, the entire universe will swiftly go to ruin.

You are the mouth of all the gods; on you the worlds are established;
and when the mortal world is abandoned by you, all of us along with Indra
shall go to utter destruction. Therefore you ought to protect us.

All the deities beginning with Indra depend on you, O Fire;
why, O venerable one, have you remained here, having abandoned the mortal world?
Why, O venerable one, do you wish to abandon us who are sinless?

Fire said:
Śakra wishes to engage me in eating uneatable things;
for that very reason this lord of the gods does not make rain in the mortal world.

Pratīpa's son was virtuous, the best of all well-behaved men;
and when Devāpi went to heaven, his younger brother Śaṃtanu,
having superseded the eldest brother, took the kingdom.
